# Copy this to `.env`. For a plain install on your own server, that is the whole of the step —
# everything below is already set, and nothing here is a credential you have to invent.
#
# Why it is this short: the three secrets are generated on first boot into `data/secrets.json`, the
# database is chosen in the browser and written to `data/database.json`, and the domains are set in
# the first-run wizard. What is left are the three things Docker Compose itself needs BEFORE any
# container exists — which is why they cannot be asked for on a web page.
#
# Everything in the "Overrides" section is optional. Setting any of it still wins over what the
# platform would do for itself, so an existing deployment that fills this in changes nothing.

# --- Required by compose, before anything runs ---------------------------------------------------

# Runs the bundled gateway and publishes a port. WITHOUT THIS NOTHING IS REACHABLE: the default
# shape assumes a reverse proxy already routes to the containers, so the stack starts healthy and
# answers nowhere. Leave it off only if something else already fronts api/admin/frontend.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=single-domain

# Where the gateway listens. `80` for a bare server. If a reverse proxy already holds 80 on this
# host, use a free port instead (e.g. `8080`), or drop the profile above and route to the containers
# directly. A bare port number binds every interface; prefix it with an address to narrow that
# (`127.0.0.1:8085` publishes to this machine only).
GATEWAY_PORT=80

# The docker network the app services join so a reverse proxy can reach them by name. It must
# already exist — compose will not create it — so for a bare install create it once:
#
#     docker network create edge
#
# Behind a proxy you already run, put ITS network here instead; `docker network ls` shows it. This
# file does not care what that proxy is. If your platform creates a network per deployment, prefer
# that one: a shared network resolves by service NAME, so two stacks both running `redis` collide.
EXTERNAL_PROXY_NETWORK=edge

# --- Overrides (all optional; set any of these and it wins) --------------------------------------

# DATABASE. Chosen in the first-run wizard and written to `data/database.json`. Set these only to
# point at a database you manage yourself — a managed PostgreSQL, or one outside this stack.
#
# THREE roles, and the separation is load-bearing. The postgres image creates POSTGRES_USER as a
# SUPERUSER; that is unavoidable, so it is spent exactly once at boot to create the other two and is
# never given to an application. A superuser bypasses row-level security entirely — connect the app
# as one and tenant isolation becomes decorative while everything still looks healthy.
#POSTGRES_USER=postgres
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
#POSTGRES_DB=fromcode
#DATABASE_BOOTSTRAP_URL=postgres://postgres:<POSTGRES_PASSWORD>@db:5432/fromcode
#DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgres://fromcode_owner:<OWNER_PASSWORD>@db:5432/fromcode
#DATABASE_URL=postgres://fromcode_app:<APP_PASSWORD>@db:5432/fromcode

# SECRETS. Generated once into `data/secrets.json` and reused from there. Set them only if you
# manage secrets elsewhere. Changing a live one is not a small thing: a new JWT_SECRET signs every
# session out, and a new INTEGRATION_SECRET_KEY makes every stored SMTP and payment credential
# permanently undecryptable. 32+ characters each, unique per deployment:
#   openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d '=+/' | cut -c1-48
#JWT_SECRET=
#INTEGRATION_SECRET_KEY=
#INTERNAL_SERVICE_SECRET=

# PUBLIC URLS. Set in the first-run wizard and changeable afterwards in Settings → General. Set them
# here only to pin them outside the admin; they must match the hostnames your proxy terminates TLS
# for.
#API_URL=https://api.example.com
#ADMIN_URL=https://admin.example.com
#FRONTEND_URL=https://example.com

#REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# Empty disables marketplace lookups. Plugin and theme updates come from their own repositories.
#MARKETPLACE_URL=

#STORAGE_DRIVER=local
#GUEST_EGRESS_POLICY=deny
#STOREFRONT_DOCUMENT_ISLANDS=false

# Set this and the gateway ALSO terminates TLS, serving the certificates the platform stores.
# Unset (the default) it terminates nothing, which is right for every deployment whose reverse proxy
# holds the certificates.
#GATEWAY_TLS_PORT=443
